#!/bin/rc

user=`{cat /dev/user}
home=/usr/$user
if(test -f $home/lib/profile){
	echo user directories already made
	exit no
}
cd $home
mkdir -p bin/rc bin/$cputype
mkdir -p lib tmp
bind -c $home/tmp /tmp

cat > lib/profile <<'!'
bind -a $home/bin/rc /bin
bind -a $home/bin/$cputype /bin
bind -c $home/tmp /tmp
test -e $home/lib/pkg && pkg add -f $home/lib/pkg
test -e $home/lib/factotum && cat $home/lib/factotum >/mnt/factotum/ctl
# $auth, the machine this page came from, serves its / to import(4): its /net, for /net/tcp, and
# webfs, and to its accounts /adm and /mnt/keys. A passkey signs you in (factotum(4)'s webauthn):
#	import ws!$auth!9p / /n/$auth; bind -a /n/$auth/net /net
font = /lib/font/bit/Iosevka/12a/font,/lib/font/bit/Iosevka/24a/font
test -e /mnt/font/Iosevka/m/r/12a/font && font = /mnt/font/Iosevka/m/r/12a/font
EDITOR=E
chessa=leela
fn cd { builtin cd $* && awd }
switch($service){
case terminal
	# sound is shared: mixfs binds over /dev/audio, so a second play mixes with the first
	if(test -e /dev/audio && ! grep -s '^mix ' /dev/volume >/dev/null)
		audio/mixfs
	plumber
	dropped &
	prompt=('% ' '	')
	fn %{ $* }
	exec hotkey rio -i riostart
}
!
if(! test -e lib/plumbing)
	cat > lib/plumbing <<!
# to update: cp /usr/$user/lib/plumbing /mnt/plumb/rules

editor = acme

include basic

!
if(! test -e lib/pkg)
	cat > lib/pkg <<!
# the packages the profile adds (pkg(1)): a name, a name and a hash, or a path, a line each
git9
!
mkdir -p lib/hotkey
if(! test -e lib/hotkey/acme)
	cat > lib/hotkey/acme <<!
# to update: cp /usr/$user/lib/hotkey/acme /mnt/hotkey/rules

include acme.emacs

!
if(! test -e intro.md)
	cat > intro.md <<'!'
# Welcome

This is plan9web, a Plan 9 system that runs in your browser. The window you
are reading is acme(1), the Plan 9 text editor, inside rio(1), the window
system. Both are Plan 9 C programs compiled to WebAssembly. They run on p9web,
a Plan 9 kernel written in TypeScript, here in this tab. Files you keep in this
directory stay in this browser.

If Plan 9 is new to you, start with intro(1), then the papers in /sys/doc:

- /sys/doc/9.ps, *Plan 9 from Bell Labs*: the system's design and the reasons for it
- /sys/doc/names.ps, *The Use of Name Spaces in Plan 9*: the core idea
- /sys/doc/acme/acme.pdf, *Acme: A User Interface for Programmers*
- /sys/doc/rc.ps, *Rc — The Plan 9 Shell*
- /sys/doc/plumb.ps, *Plumbing and Other Utilities*

To read one, click its name with button 3.

## The mouse

Plan 9 expects a mouse with three buttons. In Acme they do this:

- **Left button (B1):** select text
- **Middle button (B2):** run the text as a command
- **Right button (B3):** open the file, or search for the text

On a Mac, Option-click is B2 and Command-click is B3.

## Windows

On the background, button 3 opens rio's menu. Choose *New*, then sweep out the
new window with button 3 (on a Mac, Command-drag). To resize a window, drag its
border with button 1. To move it, drag the border with button 3.

## Commands

To run a command in Acme, type it anywhere and click it with B2. Try `ls /bin`
or `echo hello`. *New* and *Put* are commands too: B2 on *New* makes a window,
and B2 on *Put* saves the file.

The window on the right is win, which runs rc(1), the Plan 9 shell, in an Acme
window. Type a command at its end and press Return. ^C interrupts it.

B3 on a file name such as intro.md opens the file. B3 on a manual page name
such as acme(1) shows that page. In both cases the plumber(4) sends the text to
the right program. This is easier than typing `man acme`.

## Keys

Typing goes to the window under the mouse; you don't click a window before you
type. These keys act on that window:

| Key | Does |
| --- | ---- |
| Cmd-S | Put: save the file |
| Cmd-A | select the whole window |
| Cmd-P | find a file or window by name |
| Cmd-Shift-F | search the text of the files |
| Ctl-X Ctl-S, Ctl-X K | Emacs's Put and Del |

In a file that has a language server, lspfs(4) and alsp add these:

| Key | Does |
| --- | ---- |
| Cmd-B or F12 | Def: go to the definition of the word at the cursor |
| Cmd-Shift-B | Refs: list its uses |
| Cmd-I | Doc: show its documentation, which follows the cursor |
| Cmd-. | Act: the language server's fixes, then 1–9 picks, Esc cancels |
| F2 | Rename: type the new name after it in the tag, then Esc and B2 |
| Cmd-Shift-O | Syms: list the file's functions and types |
| Cmd-K, then R, I, T, D, F or C | Refs, Impls, Type, Diag, Fmt, Comp |

The keys are rules in /lib/hotkey/acme and /lib/hotkey/acme.emacs, and your
lib/hotkey/acme includes them. To add your own, put them below the include
line. A key you bind replaces the system's binding for it. To apply your
changes, run the command on that file's first line. See hotkey(1).

## Sibyl

sibyl(1) is a coding agent that works in Acme. It needs a key for the model in
factotum(4): `proto=pass service=anthropic` or `service=opencode`.

Select some text and run `|sibyl` with an instruction, such as
`|sibyl add error checks`. The answer replaces the selection, and Undo takes
it back. `win sibyl -i` holds a conversation instead. Its changes go to a copy
of the project, and *Review* in its tag shows them. *Sibyl* lists your saved
sessions.

| Key | Does |
| --- | ---- |
| Cmd-K, then E | type an instruction for the selection in the tag |
| Cmd-K, then S | Ask: put the selected lines in the sibyl session, for a question |

## Files

Drop a file or a directory on the page and it opens as if you had clicked its
name with B3:

- text opens in Acme
- a PDF or an image opens in page(1)
- an MP3, M4A, Ogg, FLAC or WAV file plays in deck(1)

Deck is a window with a scrub bar. It adds each sound you drop after it to its
playlist. Space pauses, and Exit on its button 3 menu stops it. The browser
plays sound only after you have clicked the page once.

Dropped files are kept in the drop directory here. To save a file to your
computer, run `download file`.
!

. lib/profile
